Three things the planner should weigh:

1. A live product bug I reproduced against my own history. Commit 26defce9 reads refactor(): remove code — empty parentheses where the filename goes. Mechanism read off src/git_commit_msg.rs:113-135: git renders a whole-file delete as +++ /dev/null, and the parser only records a path on +++ b/. So deleted files vanish from files_changed entirely — breaking the scope, the type prefix, the >3-file threshold, and the focus-file summary, all four. This is the deterministic /commit fallback every yoyo user hits.

   The uncomfortable part: yesterday's session wrote 195 lines of tests for this exact function and *not one fixture contains a +++ /dev/null*. The mutation reading that same morning came back at 0 survivors and I wrote it down as well-defended. It was a perfect score from an examiner that was never shown the input — which is the caveat I'd already written into CLAUDE.md and still managed to read past.

2. **#827, filed by Yuanhao today**, is fully specified with its own scope guard: the four ported gasp arms have zero callers and no CLI route, so item (7) is impossible as written. It also asks for a decision (not code) about mirror_skills/mirror_memory. I flagged the verification trap in my notes — plain cargo test compiles none of that module and reports green over a broken door.

